===Etymology 2===
From {{inh|en|enm|firen}}, {{m|enm|fyren}}, {{m|enm|furen}}, from {{inh|en|ang|fȳrian|t=to make a fire}}, from the noun (see above). Cognate with {{cog|ofs|fioria|t=to light a fire}}, {{cog|stq|fjuurje|t=to fire}}, {{cog|dum|vûren}}, {{m|enm|vueren}}, {{m|enm|vieren|t=to set fire}}, {{cog|nl|vuren|t=to fire, shoot}}, {{cog|goh|fiuren|t=to ignite, set on fire}}, {{cog|de|feuern|t=to fire}}.

====Verb====
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|transitive}} To set (something, often a building) [[on fire]].
#: {{syn|en|inflame|kindle|Thesaurus:kindle}}
#* {{RQ:Wells Invisible Man|chapter=At the House in Great Portland Street|page=186|passage=["]Then I slipped up again with a box of matches, '''fired''' my heap of paper and rubbish, put the chairs and bedding thereby, led the gas to the affair, by means of an india-rubber tube, and waving a farewell to the room left it for the last time." / "You '''fired''' the house!" exclaimed Kemp. / "'''Fired''' the house. It was the only way to cover my trail&nbsp;– and no doubt it was insured.["]}}
#* {{RQ:Haggard She|passage=That lamp was the mummy of a woman tied to a stout stake let into the rock, and he had '''fired''' her hair.}}
#* {{RQ:London Iron Heel|passage=It was long a question of debate, whether the burning of the South Side ghetto was accidental, or whether it was done by the Mercenaries; but it is definitely settled now that the ghetto was '''fired''' by the Mercenaries under orders from their chiefs.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To heat as with [[#Noun|fire]], but without setting [[on fire]], as [[ceramic]], metal objects, etc.
#: {{ux|en|If you '''fire''' the pottery at too high a temperature, it may crack.}}
#: {{ux|en|They '''fire''' the wood to make it easier to put a point on the end.}}
#* {{RQ:Ferguson Zollenstein|IV
|passage=So this was my future home, I thought! Certainly it made a brave picture. I had seen similar ones '''fired'''-in on many a Heidelberg stein. Backed by towering hills,{{...}}a sky of palest Gobelin flecked with fat, fleecy little clouds, it in truth looked a dear little city; the city of one's dreams.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To drive away by setting a [[#Noun|fire]].
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Sonnets|sonnet=144|passage=Till my bad angel '''fire''' my good one out.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|employment}} To [[terminate]] the [[employment]] contract of (an employee), especially [[for cause]] (such as [[misconduct]], [[incompetence]], or [[poor]] [[performance]]).
#: {{syn|en|dehire|dismiss|give someone their cards|give the boot|give the elbow|give someone the heave-ho|let go|make redundant|sack|terminate|throw out|unhire|Thesaurus:lay off}}
#: {{ant|en|hire}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1969|author=w:Vladimir Nabokov|title={{w|Ada or Ardor: A Family Chronicle|Ada or Ardor}}|page=226|publisher=Penguin|year_published=2011
|passage=The first, obvious choice was hysterical and fantastic Blanche – had there not been her timidity, her fear of being ‘'''fired'''’{{nb...}}.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|by extension}} To terminate a [[contract]] with a [[client]]; to [[drop]] a client.
#* {{quote-book|1=en|year=1979|author=Richard Collins Rea|title=Operating a Successful Accounting Practice: A Collection of Material from the Journal of Accountancy Practitioners Forum|chapter=|url=|page=288|isbn=9780608150970|oclc=122394178|text=Don't be hesitant to '''fire''' a client - cull out the deadwood. If a client doesn't meet the above criteria, you are better off without him. You don't do your best work for a client you'd rather not have.}}
#* {{quote-book|1=en|year=2020|author=Rebecca Migdal|title=Museum Mercenary: A Handbook for Independent Museum Professionals|chapter=|url=|page=278|isbn=9781538118603|oclc=1202618438|text=Maintaining a collegial attitude even when doing the more difficult business work, like '''firing''' a client, is another part. If you are struggling through the relationship, the client might be struggling as well, so '''firing''' them may be mutually beneficial, and you should try and do it on the best of terms.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[shoot]] (a [[gun]], [[rocket]]/[[missile]], or analogous device).
#: {{syn|en|let off|loose|q2=archery|shoot}}
#: {{ux|en|We will '''fire''' our guns at the enemy.}}
